  "image_name": "4de1b3e623ff5ac0c688659c5fa06496.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure is a schematic of the cardiac sarcomere, highlighting the main protein components of the thin and thick filaments and linking them to inherited cardiomyopathies. The central image shows two thin filaments flanking a thick filament. The thin filament is built from actin, with tropomyosin and the troponin complex arranged along it, emphasizing its role in calcium-dependent activation and relaxation. The thick filament contains myosin, including the myosin rod and light-chain region, which drives force generation and sarcomere shortening.\n\nA major focus is how specific sarcomeric proteins contribute to disease. Text boxes identify thin-filament proteins as mainly affecting calcium sensitivity, myosin heavy chain-β as altering ATPase activity or the number or availability of motor heads, myosin-binding protein C as regulating both thick and thin filaments with disease often caused by haploinsufficiency, and titin as providing passive tension and modulating contraction, with defects linked especially to dilated cardiomyopathy. The Z-disc is shown at the right, along with desmin and tropomodulin, placing the filaments in structural context. Overall, the figure conveys that different sarcomere proteins have distinct mechanical and regulatory roles, and mutations in each can lead to overlapping but mechanistically different cardiomyopathies.",
